  "query_preview": "..A fairly active pattern will continue for Southeast \nAlaska through the long term period. A significant low pressure \nsystem moving northward toward the area late Saturday and into \nSunday. This system currently appears to be the most worthy \nparticularly for the southern panhandle, with increasing forecast \nconfidence in strong winds (40-50mph) for areas south of Port \nAlexander. The uncertainty on the exact strength of this system is \ndue to how quickly and where the low that moves through on Friday \ndies out. If the Friday low weakens and dies out over by PWS before \nthe late Saturday-Sunday system moves in then the southern panhandle \ncould see sustained SE winds closer to the 45-55mph. In addition to \nwind this low will bring a round of rain and high elevations snow \n(including the Haines and Klondike Highways). This low will continue \nto move north and then west toward Prince William Sound late Sunday \nand into Monday and weaken. Multiple shortwaves after this system \nwill move through the area each brining their own bouts of \npreciptiation. What is more noteworthy during this period is the \ngradual lowering of snow levels through the rest of the week. \nCurrent guidance even has snow levels dropping down to around 1000 \nfeet with for areas along the Coastal Range and the northern \nPanhandle late next week. As a result if precipitation were to be\nalso moving through due to a shortwave disturbance there would be\nthe potential for it to fall as snow or rain/snow mix at sea \nlevel. All that said there is still great uncertainty when it \ncomes to that time frame and multiple things would have to align, \nbut it is certainly something to watch.",
